  "name": "Schelling's board: like neighbors against demand",
  "description": "Schelling's 1971 board (13 × 16, edges, eight neighbors, the nearest square, rounds) with every agent wanting the same share of like neighbors. Schelling: segregation is \"slight when the demand is for about one-third … and striking when the demand is as high as one-half\", \"a rapidly rising function of demands in the range from about 35% to 50%\". With up to eight neighbors a demand acts in eighths and sevenths, so the curve rises in small steps. Measured (release, seeds 1–20, recorded 2026-09-29), mean like share at the end: 0.50 color-blind, 0.56 at 20 %, 0.59 at 25 %, 0.66 at 30 %, 0.72 at 35–40 %, 0.80 at 45–50 %, 0.89 at 55 %, 0.91 at 60 %, 0.95 at 65 % and 0.97 at 70 %.",
